WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880
One of the things that this documentary

2
00:00:01.880 --> 00:00:06.200
has really implanted in my brain is that things take time.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.720
They take time to realize you're not in a good situation.

4
00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.680
They take time.

5
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:13.740
And one of the problems is in the legal situation

6
00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.270
is this statute of limitations.

7
00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.570
And that it's one to three years

8
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:21.410
In California, it was one to three years.

9
00:00:21.410 --> 00:00:23.252
It was.
Mm-hmm.

10
00:00:23.252 --> 00:00:27.910
You work so hard on yourself,

11
00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:31.280
but then you work so hard for other people,

12
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.640
and you go and you start this journey

13
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:35.930
of legally trying to change the system

14
00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:39.190
because you so eloquently explain

15
00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.010
that it takes time for people to realize

16
00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.480
that they've been a victim,

17
00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.620
that they've been gaslit, to have the bravery.

18
00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:50.290
You have written now what is called the Phoenix Act

19
00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:54.180
and you have changed legislation in California,

20
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.123
which is incredible.

21
00:00:57.347 --> 00:00:58.326
Yeah.

22
00:00:58.326 --> 00:01:01.493
(audience applauding)

23
00:01:03.233 --> 00:01:06.233
What was the moment that you said,

24
00:01:07.177 --> 00:01:09.580
"I am willing to do this

25
00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:12.480
not just for myself, but other people?"

26
00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.350
It was when I found out there were other people,

27
00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:16.160
and this is the part that actually

28
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.300
makes me the most emotional is when I talk

29
00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.120
about how desperately people need help.

30
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.657
When I first started speaking about my abuse,

31
00:01:27.657 --> 00:01:29.910
I was still sort of in the midst of processing it.

32
00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.270
When I testified in front of Congress

33
00:01:32.270 --> 00:01:34.100
for another bill for the sexual assault survivor's

34
00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.490
bill of rights, I thought that was gonna be it,

35
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:36.580
I thought that was gonna be the only time

36
00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.600
I told my story, thought was gonna leave it there,

37
00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:39.800
walk away, move on.

38
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.507
And then I saw, after I testified,

39
00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:45.420
all these stories popping up on the internet,

40
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.420
and when I saw that, it was like a bomb went off

41
00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.814
and I could tell by the details in their stories,

42
00:01:52.814 --> 00:01:55.230
they knew things that nobody else knew.

43
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.640
I knew they were telling the truth.

44
00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.951
And there was this amazing moment of liberation,

45
00:02:01.951 --> 00:02:05.240
I'm not crazy.

46
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.070
All of the thing, the way this person gaslit me,

47
00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:11.860
anytime I question them, anytime I challenge them,

48
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:14.680
the way they made me feel like I was so wrong,

49
00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.750
and I was so unhinged.

50
00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.750
And then to see that I was right,

51
00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.540
like I can trust myself.

52
00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:27.618
I wasn't, I see now the truth.

53
00:02:27.618 --> 00:02:30.285 line:15%
(upbeat music)

